## Verdict

SOLVED: no

Best current status: exact value characterization is available for finite public-exogenous classes, and there is a plausible exact arbitrary-class extension using finitely additive priors. But the original tail-characterization/rate problem is not solved: no scalar tail iff, no matching power-tail lower rate, and no arbitrary infinite-class ordinary-Bayes theorem without extra no-gap hypotheses.

## Candidate Solution Or Main Attempt

For finite horizon \(T\), finite grid \(G\), public exogenous class \(\Theta\), define
\[
M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s
\]
and
\[
V_\theta^G=\max_{a\in G}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb E_\theta[a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\}].
\]

For finite \(\Theta\), the accepted exact characterization is
\[
\mathcal R_T^G
=
\inf_A\sup_{\theta\in\Theta}(V_\theta^G-L_\theta(A))
=
\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta)}\Psi_G(\pi),
\]
where
\[
\Psi_G(\pi)
=
\sum_\theta \pi_\theta V_\theta^G
-
\sum_t\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}\max_{a\in G}
a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t}).
\]

For arbitrary \(\Theta\), a stronger but less probabilistic candidate is the finitely additive dual:
\[
\mathcal R_T^G
=
\sup_{\nu\in ba_1^+(\Theta)}
\left\{
\langle V^G,\nu\rangle
-
\sup_A \langle L(A),\nu\rangle
\right\}.
\]
Here \(ba_1^+(\Theta)\) is the set of positive finitely additive probability measures on \(\Theta\). This appears to give an exact necessary-and-sufficient minimax value condition for arbitrary classes, but it loses the ordinary posterior/Bayes interpretation.

With \(G_T=\{0,1/T,\ldots,(T-1)/T\}\),
\[
|(\mathcal R_T^{[0,1]})_+-(\mathcal R_T^{G_T})_+|\le 1.
\]
Thus sublinear continuum upper regret is equivalent to the positive part of the grid value being \(o(T)\).

## Concrete Lemmas Or Reductions

1. Truncation upper bound:
\[
R_T\lesssim \sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}+T/K+\sup_{\theta}\delta_T^\theta(H).
\]
If \(\delta_T(H)\le T H^{-\alpha}\), optimizing gives
\[
R_T\lesssim
T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}
(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}.
\]

2. Known-law oracle:
for every known finite-horizon exogenous law, conditional grid maximization gives \(R_T\le T/K\), hence \(O(1)\) with \(K=T\).

3. Hidden-terminal atom:
two indistinguishable worlds with terminal mass \(q\) after \(D\) rounds force
\[
\inf_A\sup_\theta R_T^\theta(A)\ge qD/8.
\]

4. Last-exceedance identity:
\[
r_t(a)=a{\bf 1}\{L_a>t\},\qquad
\sum_t r_t(a)=a(L_a-1)_+.
\]
This clarifies why raw first-hitting tails are not the right value object.

## Gaps And Failure Points

The finitely additive dual needs critic verification, especially the separation argument and policy-class convexity assumptions.

Even if correct, it is a game-value characterization, not a usable scalar tail condition.

The ordinary-prior infinite-class theorem still needs product-closure, payoff-measure domination, or another no-gap assumption.

Matching power-tail lower bounds remain open. The one-block \(qD\) construction is too weak.

Tail-adaptive truncation remains conditional on an unproved comparator-specific heterogeneous-delay experts theorem.

## Counterexamples Or Obstructions

Raw \(\beta(h)\to0\) is not necessary: known laws, iid uniform prices, tiny-price long-delay blocks, and rare-history processes refute it.

Per-world \(\beta,\Delta,\delta\) profiles cannot characterize unknown-class minimax learnability: the hidden/labeled terminal pair has identical per-law profiles but regret \(qD/8\) versus zero.

Finite-subclass priors do not characterize arbitrary infinite classes without no-gap assumptions; the non-Borel label example gives a linear Borel-policy gap.

Naive repeated hidden-terminal atoms fail because old quotes persist, shared types get revealed, and independent bits collapse against one fixed comparator.

## Promising Ideas To Explore

Formalize or reject the finitely additive dual. If accepted, it gives the cleanest arbitrary-class iff, albeit not a tail theorem.

For ordinary probability characterizations, finish the product-closure/payoff-measure dominated theorem package.

For lower rates, design limited-leakage or codebook constructions and compute their finite-grid \(\Psi_G\) value before realizing them as price processes.

For adaptive upper bounds, solve the two-arm delayed-advantage game before returning to market truncation windows.

## Notes For Critics

Check horizon-censoring everywhere: \(M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s\).

Keep finite-grid learner actions, comparator, and Bayes envelope on the same grid.

Use positive-part upper regret; adaptive learners can beat the best fixed quote.

Do not promote the finitely additive dual as an ordinary Bayes/posterior characterization.
